# A client that names the address it asks shows where its request goes: the clean Rust `item` asks
# `GET /api/items/{id}` of the machine the parts run on, `localhost:8000`, where `shop.api.item`, another part of the
# program, serves it. What that answers is what `item` gives back, so the place stands in the route's domain. The
# client it asks through is the caller's, a value from the route's start. (The shortcut's `item` asks an address the
# code does not say: its place is `Web`, with none.)

route rsclean.item
line entry > item call
line Client·client > item value                                   # `client.get(..).send()?.text()`
line Web(localhost:8000) > item own
line item > _@item value                                         # `send()?`, in its own code: what `?` hands on, a reqwest error

home Client·client item
home _@item item

side entry left
side Web(localhost:8000) left

# A request's method says which way it goes, as a statement's verb does: the clean `order` sends `POST /api/orders`,
# which leaves something at the place, and drops what it answers (`send()?;`), so the place stands in the codomain.
# (The shortcut's `order` looks its method up by a key at run time, `client.request(method("order"), ..)`: the code
# does not say which way it goes, and the place is in neither.)

route rsclean.order
line entry > order call
line Client·client > order value                                  # `client.post(..).body(..).send()?`
line order > Web(localhost:8000) own
line order > _@order value                                       # `send()?;`, in its own code

home Client·client order
home _@order order

side entry left
side Web(localhost:8000) right
